\section{Type Both Values and Temporal Behavior}

Lucid Synchrone combines synchronous streams with ML-style functions, algebraic data, pattern matching, and higher-order programming. Ordinary \texttt{let} definitions are combinational; \texttt{let node} marks sequential stream functions that may depend on history. The compiler infers value types and clock types, and separately checks initialization and causality \cite{pouzet2006lucidsynchrone}.

A clock type describes presence, not an execution frequency in hertz. If \(x\) and boolean \(c\) have base clock \(\alpha\), then \texttt{x when c} has subclock \(\alpha\ \texttt{on}\ c\). Sampling the input of a stateful function is generally not equivalent to sampling its output, because the former changes how often state advances.

\section{Define and Sample a Stream Function}

Real Lucid Synchrone syntax distinguishes the stateful node:

\begin{verbatim}
let node sum x = s where
  rec s = x -> pre s + x

let node sampled_sum x c =
  sum (x when c)
\end{verbatim}

\texttt{sum} has one initialized delay. In \texttt{sampled\_sum}, that delay advances only when \(c\) is present and true. By contrast, \verb|(sum x) when c| computes the base-clock running sum and merely hides some outputs. \Cref{fig:visual-365} shows why the two placements of \texttt{when} differ.

